robin <news@unrated.net> deserves a cookie for saying:
> In message <bo90hn$o2n$1@news-int.gatech.edu>, Bruce wrote:
>> But isn't this, by nature, an arbitrary distinction?  You could just as
>> easily decide that the overall ascension trophies are "standard" and the
>> per-class tropies are "special."  Or that they're all "standard."  

> Not really; NetHack itself is written to use just the scores as a way
> of ranking players.  The "standard" ranking in NetHack is merely raw
> highest score which, in order to have more trophies and reflect one
> aspect of the game's complexity, we expanded to raw highest score
> sorted into classes.

Yes, but why does nethack use the score to rank?  The general opinion of
those who have ascended several times or more seems to be that score
only matters before ascension.  To that extent, the scoring system is
very effective.  It efficiently and accurately (enough) ranks the
ability of each player.

Once ascensions enter the mix, though, it falls apart.  High scores no
longer reflect skill.  In fact, the opposite is frequently true.  To
this extent, the scoring system is flawed.  

That's all well and good in our everyday play, as we understand that
conducts and consistency are the real measures of talent and score means
very little.  However, in a tournament that, purportedly, is meant to
reward skill, awarding most of the prizes based on a flawed scoring
system doesn't make sense.  

For instance, do any of us believe that someone who gets the Highest
Scoring Caveman Ascension with 157 million points is really anywhere
near as good as Marvin, who might rack up 16 ascensions?  Do we even
think they're as good as the person who comes in second with 14
ascensions?  No, of course not.  But that person isn't going to get a
trophy, whereas the person who kills Demo a thousand times will.  

(Note: Said other people would not be me, so I'm not arguing for my own
 benefit here.)

> The other three trophies were added to reflect other aspects of
> NetHack's complexity.  In general, if we're going to add tropies, we'd
> prefer to add new ones which will broaden the areas of the game
> reflected in the trophies.  Next year, for example, we'll probably run
> the Tournament with the conducts activated and start to add trophies
> for highest score with various conducts.

And that's wonderful.  It's the aspect of the game that truly judges
skill.  

>> There doesn't seem to be a compelling basis for ranking the per-class
